{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775350300-YwnsEHoVkGCGx5SXYgeb","role":"assistant","created":1775350309,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Looking at the data, I need to find when the Treasury notes of 1890 were removed from the U.S. federal government ledgers. Looking at the footnotes:\n\nFrom the 1962 bulletin (page 82):\n\"The Treasury notes of 1890 have been in process of retirement since March 1900\"\n\"The Secretary of the Treasury determined on October 20, 1961, that $1 million of Treasury notes of 1890 have been destroyed or irretrievably lost and so will never be presented for redemption.\"\n\nFrom the 1963 bulletin (page 88):\n\"Accordingly, the Secretary of the Treasury has determined that the following paper currencies have been destroyed or irretreiably lost and so will never be presented for redemption: On October 20, 1961, $1 million of Treasury notes of 1890;\"\n\nWait, I need to think about this more carefully. The question asks: \"In the calendar year that the treasury notes of 1890 were removed from the U.S federal government ledgers, how much paper money was added in circulation?\"\n\nSo I need to find:\n1. What year the treasury notes of 1890 were removed from the ledgers\n2. How much paper money was added in circulation in that year\n\nLooking at the data more carefully, it seems like the treasury notes of 1890 were being retired over time. The footnotes mention \"The Treasury notes of 1890 have been in process of retirement since March 1900\" - but this refers to the process, not the final removal.\n\nLooking at the tables:\n- In 1961: Treasury notes of 1890 = 2,094 (for June 1961), 2,077 (July), 2,075 (August), 2,079 (September), 2,076 (October), 2,098 (November)\n- In 1962: Treasury notes of 1890 = 2,009 (June), 1,980 (July), 1,945 (August), 1,943 (September), 1,947 (October), 1,957 (November)\n\n\n- The footnotes indicate that on October 20, 1961, the Treasury determined $1 million of Treasury notes of 1890 were destroyed, marking their official removal from government records.\n\nI'll analyze the paper money circulation changes between 1961 and 1962. The total paper money increased from 29,943 million to 31,117 million, representing an addition of 1,174 million dollars in circulation during the year the Treasury notes were officially removed.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_3799893f31c555b2","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"todo__todo_write","arguments":{"content":"- [x] Find when treasury notes of 1890 were removed from U.S. federal government ledgers\n- [x] Find how much paper money was added in circulation in that year\n- [ ] Report answer in millions of nominal dollars to /app/answer.txt"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"todo"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
